1
00:00:01,220 --> 00:00:03,775
Tonight, you will lie down, lose consciousness,

2
00:00:03,795 --> 00:00:05,653
and barely move for hours.

3
00:00:06,623 --> 00:00:07,613
For part of the night,

4
00:00:07,743 --> 00:00:09,473
your muscles will be switched off,

5
00:00:09,603 --> 00:00:10,861
and your brain will show you

6
00:00:10,881 --> 00:00:12,371
things that are not there.

7
00:00:13,311 --> 00:00:15,161
You do this every single night.

8
00:00:15,341 --> 00:00:16,031
Add it up,

9
00:00:16,051 --> 00:00:16,601
and it's about a third of your life.

10
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:17,611
Over eighty years,

11
00:00:17,901 --> 00:00:21,201
that's roughly twenty-six years asleep.

12
00:00:22,071 --> 00:00:23,251
And it's not just you.

13
00:00:23,271 --> 00:00:24,391
Elephants do it.

14
00:00:24,481 --> 00:00:25,491
Penguins do it.

15
00:00:25,701 --> 00:00:28,075
Even a jellyfish with no brain does it.

16
00:00:28,805 --> 00:00:31,665
In the wild,

17
00:00:31,705 --> 00:00:32,986
lying still with your eyes shut is about

18
00:00:33,006 --> 00:00:34,585
the most dangerous thing an animal can do.

19
00:00:34,925 --> 00:00:37,003
Yet evolution never got rid of it.

20
00:00:37,843 --> 00:00:39,653
So, why do we have to sleep?

21
00:00:46,383 --> 00:00:47,243
Chapter one.

22
00:00:47,263 --> 00:00:48,779
The boy who stayed awake.

23
00:00:49,469 --> 00:00:52,005
What happens if you simply refuse to sleep?

24
00:00:52,445 --> 00:00:54,405
In December nineteen sixty-three,

25
00:00:54,615 --> 00:00:56,927
a seventeen-year-old from San Diego named

26
00:00:56,947 --> 00:00:59,225
Randy Gardner decided to find out,

27
00:00:59,505 --> 00:01:01,081
for a school science fair.

28
00:01:01,801 --> 00:01:04,381
Two friends kept him awake and ran the tests.

29
00:01:04,681 --> 00:01:07,511
A Stanford sleep researcher, William Dement,

30
00:01:07,531 --> 00:01:08,623
came to watch.

31
00:01:09,053 --> 00:01:09,893
On day ten,

32
00:01:09,913 --> 00:01:11,033
the story goes,

33
00:01:11,103 --> 00:01:12,943
Randy still beat him at pinball.

34
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:15,573
As the days passed,

35
00:01:15,593 --> 00:01:20,273
a Navy doctor recorded moodiness, trouble concentrating, memory lapses,

36
00:01:20,293 --> 00:01:21,959
and even hallucinations.

37
00:01:22,799 --> 00:01:25,829
Randy lasted eleven days and twenty-five minutes.

38
00:01:26,059 --> 00:01:28,833
Two hundred and sixty-four hours without sleep.

39
00:01:29,903 --> 00:01:32,383
Then he slept for almost fifteen hours straight,

40
00:01:32,503 --> 00:01:34,286
and doctors found him healthy.

41
00:01:35,126 --> 00:01:36,716
Rats were not so lucky.

42
00:01:37,006 --> 00:01:39,056
In experiments in the nineteen eighties,

43
00:01:39,106 --> 00:01:41,456
rats kept totally awake all died,

44
00:01:41,486 --> 00:01:42,776
or came close to death,

45
00:01:42,836 --> 00:01:44,786
within eleven to thirty-two days.

46
00:01:45,096 --> 00:01:47,069
Nobody could find a clear cause.

47
00:01:47,899 --> 00:01:49,189
In nineteen ninety-seven,

48
00:01:49,209 --> 00:01:50,149
Guinness stopped accepting this record.

49
00:01:50,169 --> 00:01:51,109
Too dangerous.

50
00:01:51,339 --> 00:01:54,007
Please, don't try it.

51
00:01:54,747 --> 00:01:56,477
So sleep is not optional.

52
00:01:56,607 --> 00:01:58,200
But what is it actually for?

53
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:00,740
Chapter two.

54
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:02,276
Two clocks inside you.

55
00:02:02,966 --> 00:02:05,056
Why do you get sleepy in the first place?

56
00:02:05,146 --> 00:02:07,400
Because two clocks are ticking inside you.

57
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:09,860
The first works like an hourglass.

58
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:11,500
Every hour you are awake,

59
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:14,560
a chemical called adenosine builds up in your brain.

60
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:15,670
The more of it,

61
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:17,074
the sleepier you feel.

62
00:02:17,824 --> 00:02:19,604
Sleep empties the hourglass.

63
00:02:19,734 --> 00:02:20,764
Coffee doesn't.

64
00:02:21,064 --> 00:02:24,474
Caffeine only blocks the sensors that detect adenosine,

65
00:02:24,524 --> 00:02:27,219
so you stop feeling a pressure that is still there.

66
00:02:28,159 --> 00:02:30,039
The second is a real clock.

67
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:32,195
Deep in your brain sits a cluster of

68
00:02:32,215 --> 00:02:34,909
about twenty thousand nerve cells that keeps time,

69
00:02:35,039 --> 00:02:36,689
even in total darkness.

70
00:02:37,119 --> 00:02:38,389
As evening comes,

71
00:02:38,439 --> 00:02:40,679
it tells your brain to release melatonin,

72
00:02:40,829 --> 00:02:42,715
the signal that night has begun.

73
00:02:43,565 --> 00:02:45,825
Left alone, it runs a little slow.

74
00:02:46,045 --> 00:02:49,135
One day lasts about twenty-four hours and eleven minutes.

75
00:02:49,415 --> 00:02:50,855
Morning light resets it,

76
00:02:50,875 --> 00:02:52,149
every single day.

77
00:02:53,119 --> 00:02:54,299
Fly across the world,

78
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:56,289
and that clock stays on the old time.

79
00:02:56,479 --> 00:02:57,779
That is jet lag.

80
00:02:58,709 --> 00:02:59,759
When the hourglass is full,

81
00:02:59,779 --> 00:03:01,299
and the clock says night,

82
00:03:01,319 --> 00:03:02,259
the two line up.

83
00:03:02,459 --> 00:03:03,686
And you fall asleep.

84
00:03:05,756 --> 00:03:06,676
Chapter three.

85
00:03:06,696 --> 00:03:08,290
What a night really looks like.

86
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:10,980
Is sleep just your brain switching off?

87
00:03:11,090 --> 00:03:12,219
Not even close.

88
00:03:12,669 --> 00:03:14,829
A night of sleep moves in cycles,

89
00:03:14,849 --> 00:03:16,789
each roughly ninety minutes long.

90
00:03:17,059 --> 00:03:19,389
Most people go through four to six of them.

91
00:03:19,679 --> 00:03:21,499
Scientists can watch it happen,

92
00:03:21,549 --> 00:03:24,027
by reading the brain's electrical waves.

93
00:03:24,957 --> 00:03:29,857
First, you sink down into deep sleep.

94
00:03:29,937 --> 00:03:31,227
Your brain waves turn slow and huge,

95
00:03:31,247 --> 00:03:32,497
your heart rate drops,

96
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:32,906
and you are very hard to wake.

97
00:03:33,776 --> 00:03:36,286
Then you rise back up,

98
00:03:36,506 --> 00:03:37,866
into something strange.

99
00:03:37,996 --> 00:03:39,456
REM sleep.

100
00:03:39,646 --> 00:03:42,286
Your eyes dart around.

101
00:03:42,306 --> 00:03:42,309
Your brain is almost as busy

102
00:03:42,329 --> 00:03:42,596
as when you are awake.

103
00:03:42,886 --> 00:03:45,327
This is where your most vivid dreams happen.

104
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:49,187
And your arms and legs are temporarily paralysed,

105
00:03:49,287 --> 00:03:51,106
so you don't act those dreams out.

106
00:03:52,046 --> 00:03:53,467
Deep sleep comes mostly in the

107
00:03:53,487 --> 00:03:54,866
first half of the night.

108
00:03:55,126 --> 00:03:57,326
Dream sleep takes over toward morning.

109
00:03:57,556 --> 00:03:58,696
Cut the night short,

110
00:03:58,806 --> 00:04:00,789
and it's mostly dreams you lose.

111
00:04:02,769 --> 00:04:03,629
Chapter four.

112
00:04:03,649 --> 00:04:04,781
The night shift.

113
00:04:05,491 --> 00:04:06,671
While you are out cold,

114
00:04:06,691 --> 00:04:08,201
a night shift clocks in.

115
00:04:08,371 --> 00:04:09,799
And it has a lot to do.

116
00:04:10,239 --> 00:04:11,419
Start with the body.

117
00:04:11,739 --> 00:04:12,639
In deep sleep,

118
00:04:12,749 --> 00:04:14,494
your body releases a large share

119
00:04:14,514 --> 00:04:16,259
of its daily growth hormone,

120
00:04:16,379 --> 00:04:18,342
the signal to build and repair.

121
00:04:19,182 --> 00:04:21,272
Your immune system counts on it too.

122
00:04:21,592 --> 00:04:22,812
In one experiment,

123
00:04:22,902 --> 00:04:26,732
a hundred and sixty-four volunteers were given a cold virus.

124
00:04:27,052 --> 00:04:29,379
Those who slept less than six hours a night

125
00:04:29,399 --> 00:04:31,814
were about four times more likely to get sick

126
00:04:31,834 --> 00:04:34,133
than those who slept more than seven.

127
00:04:35,063 --> 00:04:35,843
And the brain?

128
00:04:35,863 --> 00:04:36,993
In twenty thirteen,

129
00:04:37,013 --> 00:04:39,213
a study in mice found that during sleep,

130
00:04:39,273 --> 00:04:42,533
the space between brain cells grows by sixty percent,

131
00:04:42,583 --> 00:04:44,663
letting fluid wash waste away.

132
00:04:45,063 --> 00:04:46,843
The idea became famous.

133
00:04:47,043 --> 00:04:48,851
Sleep as a brain wash.

134
00:04:49,781 --> 00:04:51,391
But science isn't finished.

135
00:04:51,641 --> 00:04:52,931
In twenty twenty-four,

136
00:04:52,951 --> 00:04:54,901
another team measured the opposite.

137
00:04:55,141 --> 00:04:56,311
In sleeping mice,

138
00:04:56,331 --> 00:04:57,301
clearance was about thirty percent lower.

139
00:04:57,321 --> 00:05:02,545
Right now, the brain wash idea is honestly under debate.

140
00:05:03,595 --> 00:05:04,805
What is not in doubt:

141
00:05:04,955 --> 00:05:06,935
in studies of flies and mice,

142
00:05:06,965 --> 00:05:10,395
losing sleep lets harmful molecules build up in the gut.

143
00:05:10,805 --> 00:05:12,765
Sleep loss hurts the whole body,

144
00:05:12,785 --> 00:05:14,078
not just the brain.

145
00:05:15,958 --> 00:05:16,858
Chapter five.

146
00:05:16,878 --> 00:05:18,055
Saving the day.

147
00:05:18,775 --> 00:05:20,235
Why does a problem look easier

148
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:21,729
after a night of sleep?

149
00:05:22,159 --> 00:05:23,499
In nineteen ninety-four,

150
00:05:23,569 --> 00:05:27,339
scientists recorded brain cells in rats as they explored.

151
00:05:27,729 --> 00:05:29,370
Each place the rat visited made

152
00:05:29,390 --> 00:05:31,147
its own set of cells fire.

153
00:05:32,087 --> 00:05:33,688
Later, while the rats slept,

154
00:05:33,708 --> 00:05:35,815
the same cells fired together again.

155
00:05:35,835 --> 00:05:38,611
The sleeping brain was replaying the day.

156
00:05:40,341 --> 00:05:42,150
That replay is thought to help

157
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:44,871
move fresh memories into long-term storage.

158
00:05:45,301 --> 00:05:47,471
It may be part of why newborn babies,

159
00:05:47,551 --> 00:05:49,331
who are learning everything at once,

160
00:05:49,451 --> 00:05:51,793
sleep fourteen to seventeen hours a day.

161
00:05:52,653 --> 00:05:54,053
There is a second idea.

162
00:05:54,413 --> 00:05:56,415
Learning all day makes the connections

163
00:05:56,435 --> 00:05:58,753
between brain cells bigger and stronger,

164
00:05:58,893 --> 00:06:00,563
and that can't go on forever.

165
00:06:00,973 --> 00:06:01,773
In mice,

166
00:06:01,793 --> 00:06:03,270
a few hours of sleep shrank

167
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:05,743
those connections by about eighteen percent,

168
00:06:05,883 --> 00:06:08,501
turning down the noise so the next day has room.

169
00:06:09,451 --> 00:06:12,281
And it has been measured since nineteen twenty-four.

170
00:06:12,601 --> 00:06:14,729
People remember more after sleeping than

171
00:06:14,749 --> 00:06:16,563
after the same hours awake.

172
00:06:17,403 --> 00:06:18,319
So, sleep on it,

173
00:06:18,339 --> 00:06:19,206
is real advice.

174
00:06:19,226 --> 00:06:21,688
Your brain does its filing at night.

175
00:06:23,678 --> 00:06:24,618
Chapter six.

176
00:06:24,638 --> 00:06:26,171
Sleeping dangerously.

177
00:06:26,851 --> 00:06:28,431
If sleep is so risky,

178
00:06:28,481 --> 00:06:30,601
how do wild animals get away with it?

179
00:06:30,851 --> 00:06:32,831
Some of their tricks are unbelievable.

180
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:35,751
Dolphins sleep with half a brain at a time.

181
00:06:36,051 --> 00:06:37,161
One half rests,

182
00:06:37,181 --> 00:06:38,751
while the other keeps them swimming,

183
00:06:38,771 --> 00:06:40,179
and coming up to breathe.

184
00:06:41,149 --> 00:06:44,339
Frigatebirds fly over the ocean for weeks without landing.

185
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:45,969
They sleep in the air,

186
00:06:46,039 --> 00:06:48,109
only about forty-two minutes a day,

187
00:06:48,129 --> 00:06:49,351
in short bursts.

188
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:52,444
Nesting chinstrap penguins take more than

189
00:06:52,464 --> 00:06:54,021
ten thousand naps a day,

190
00:06:54,131 --> 00:06:55,841
each about four seconds long.

191
00:06:56,131 --> 00:06:58,138
It adds up to eleven hours.

192
00:06:59,188 --> 00:07:02,138
Wild elephants get by on about two hours,

193
00:07:02,198 --> 00:07:03,748
the least of any mammal measured.

194
00:07:04,068 --> 00:07:07,047
A little brown bat in a lab slept almost twenty.

195
00:07:07,987 --> 00:07:10,877
And the upside-down jellyfish has no brain at all.

196
00:07:11,167 --> 00:07:12,657
Yet it goes quiet at night,

197
00:07:12,697 --> 00:07:14,618
and gets sluggish if you keep it up.

198
00:07:15,648 --> 00:07:18,718
Every animal studied closely pays the price of sleep,

199
00:07:18,808 --> 00:07:20,308
one way or another.

200
00:07:20,788 --> 00:07:23,888
The sleep scientist Allan Rechtschaffen liked to say:

201
00:07:24,158 --> 00:07:27,398
if sleep does not serve an absolutely vital function,

202
00:07:27,508 --> 00:07:30,673
then it is the biggest mistake evolution ever made.

203
00:07:32,113 --> 00:07:33,923
So, why do we have to sleep?

204
00:07:34,363 --> 00:07:35,713
There isn't one answer.

205
00:07:36,033 --> 00:07:37,503
Sleep repairs the body,

206
00:07:37,523 --> 00:07:38,993
backs up the immune system,

207
00:07:39,053 --> 00:07:40,483
files away memories,

208
00:07:40,503 --> 00:07:42,573
and resets the brain for another day.

209
00:07:43,513 --> 00:07:45,453
And scientists keep finding more.

210
00:07:45,873 --> 00:07:46,823
Just this month,

211
00:07:47,003 --> 00:07:49,888
a review of fifty-seven brain scan studies linked

212
00:07:49,908 --> 00:07:53,174
sleep disorders to changes deep inside the brain.

213
00:07:54,014 --> 00:07:58,474
Skip sleep, and you pay fast.

214
00:07:58,594 --> 00:08:00,854
After seventeen to nineteen hours awake,

215
00:08:00,874 --> 00:08:02,250
people did as badly on some tests

216
00:08:02,270 --> 00:08:03,612
as they did with a blood alcohol

217
00:08:03,632 --> 00:08:05,509
level of zero point zero five percent.

218
00:08:06,489 --> 00:08:07,519
For most adults,

219
00:08:07,539 --> 00:08:09,859
the target is at least seven hours a night.

220
00:08:10,609 --> 00:08:12,579
That third of your life isn't wasted.

221
00:08:12,789 --> 00:08:15,055
It is what makes the other two thirds work.

222
00:08:16,385 --> 00:08:17,395
If you enjoyed this,

223
00:08:17,415 --> 00:08:19,705
give it a like,

224
00:08:19,925 --> 00:08:21,305
and subscribe to Whyverse.

225
00:08:21,325 --> 00:08:22,875
And for shorter stories,

226
00:08:22,895 --> 00:08:23,505
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

227
00:08:23,715 --> 00:08:24,376
Sleep well.

